From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Darren Shepherd <darren@obot.ai>
Date: Thu, 6 Aug 2026 07:21:45 -0700
Subject: [PATCH] feat(control): search on a ghostty pane

The needle, the count and the navigation all work; what needed designing was
the seam. IControlCore::Search is synchronous - Windows Terminal asks and
expects the answer in the return value - while ghostty searches on a thread of
its own and reports the counts back later, as SEARCH_TOTAL and SEARCH_SELECTED
actions, after the call has already returned.

So Search() starts the work and returns what it knows. For a new needle that is
nothing, and it says so with a negative total rather than a zero: SearchBoxControl
renders a negative total as "Searching...", so the honest answer and the
right-looking one are the same answer, where zero would claim there are no
matches for a search that has not run. SearchResultsChanged brings TermControl
back round to ask again when the real numbers land - it already re-queries on
OutputIdle, but a search typed into a quiet terminal produces no output to be
idle after.

The regex and case toggles are disabled, not ignored. ghostty's needle is a
plain byte string, so SearchSupportsRegex/SearchSupportsCaseSensitivity report
what each engine can honour and the search box turns off what it cannot - and
unchecks it, because a toggle left lit while its meaning is gone is the same
lie in a quieter form.

Scrollbar pips stay empty rather than wrong: ghostty reports counts, not match
positions, and a partial list would draw pips in the wrong places. Documented
diff.

Proven end to end in scripts/smoke-harness.ps1, which searches a fixture with
three occurrences and walks next/next/previous - the counts arriving late,
through the app tick, exactly as they do in a pane. Five unit tests cover what
Search() returns while it waits.

WT suites: terminalCore 54/54, unitSettingsModel 159/159, unitControl 60/60,
terminalApp 51/51.

